## Context

At Cimory Group, overtime was handled through chat messages and manual Excel recaps. Overtime management is not only about recording working hours: it involves submission, location verification, multi-level approvals, notifications, and reporting that has to be consistent and traceable.

## Problem

Before the system, HR had to request tap-in and tap-out data from HR Ops, follow up with employees on null records, and look for supporting proof for each null entry. Each recap took about 4 hours.

## My role

Owner: I designed and developed the system end to end, from process mapping, database design, and backend logic to frontend development and deployment.

## As-is analysis

I mapped the process end to end across overtime submission, attendance location verification, organizational approval hierarchy, HR administration, and final payroll recap. Most rework came from missing attendance punches, informal chat approvals, and manual cross-checking across spreadsheets.

## Requirements

I translated the operational rules into explicit system logic:

- Location check: GPS coordinates validated against a 100-meter geofence around the office.
- Approval routing by organizational structure, with flexibility for specific exception cases.
- Approve or reject requests directly from email using signed tokens.
- Revision requests when details or proof of work need correction.
- Automatic rejection of overtime with an actual duration under one hour, notifying the employee and informing the manager.

## Solution design

The architecture models overtime requests as a state-driven approval workflow backed by PostgreSQL, pairing GPS geofencing on submission with signed-token email actions so managers can approve quickly without losing auditability. Supporting evidence photos are stored in Supabase Storage, while finalized records are exported as HR-ready Excel recaps and hash-verified PDF documents.

## Build

The system is built with Node.js, Express, PostgreSQL, HTML/JavaScript, and Tailwind CSS, with Supabase Storage for proof-of-work uploads, the Brevo API for transactional email notifications, and deployment across Cloudflare Pages and Render (`overtime-demo-tci7.onrender.com`). Source code: [github.com/thunderclouds863/overtime-system](https://github.com/thunderclouds863/overtime-system).

## Verification

Verified the geofence validation boundary, multi-level organizational routing, signed email approve/reject links, under-one-hour auto-rejection rule, and automated Excel/PDF export outputs before deployment.

## Outcome

HR's overtime recap time went from about 4 hours to about 5 minutes (around 95% reduction), because attendance validation, approvals, and recap generation are now handled automatically in one system.

## Reflection

Building this system meant understanding the business process, translating operational rules into system logic, designing dynamic approval workflows, and protecting data security and traceability. It reinforced a core principle: people analytics is not only about analyzing available data, it is also about improving the processes that produce that data.
